WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.770
- Hi, I'm Seth Juarez,
and I'm having a conversation today

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.510
with doctors Krysta

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.700
Svore and Nathan Wiebe,
researchers at Microsoft Research

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.610
who have been pushing
the boundaries

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.910
of artificial intelligence
through quantum machine learning.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.620
Welcome, Krysta and Nathan.
How you doing?

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.240
- Thanks a lot, Seth.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.590
Doing great.
- Fantastic.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.970
So, the first question
is probably the most one.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.940
We've all been hearing a lot about quantum
computing in the news lately.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.030
How is Microsoft working to make
the dream of quantum computing a reality?

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:36.900
Start with you, Krysta.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:37.560
- OK, great.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.790
Yeah, so Microsoft, we've actually
been working on quantum

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.140
computing for over a decade,

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.260
and we've been looking to really build

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.580
and create a scalable,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.040
general-purpose quantum computer.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.930
We're taking a really distinct,
unique, bold approach,

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.620
and we're working on a type of qubit
called a topological qubit,

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.470
which is very different
than the other types

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.880
of qubits being pursued,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.540
such as super-conducting qubits,
ion-trap qubits, and so forth.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.000
And I think the other piece
about our approach that's really unique

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.430
is that it's really a full-stack,
full-system approach.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.740
So, we're working on the hardware layer,
the software layer,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.600
and then also, what are you going
to do with the quantum computer?

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.270
So, the application layer.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.240
- I see.
- Well, yeah,

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.160
I guess on the application
side of things,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.880
really one of the things
that we've found is that quantum

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.660
computing really promises
to totally revolutionize

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.200
the way that we approach
artificial intelligence

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.440
and machine learning.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.010
And the way that it will do this
is by leveraging quantum effects,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.130
such as entanglement
and interference,

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.690
to solve currently unsolvable problems
in machine learning.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.220
And it isn't actually
just speeding up.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:54.960
The real thing that actually it ends up
giving us is actually also more rich models for data

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.770
than what we would normally be able
to have using conventional approaches.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:58.640
- I see.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.190
Keep going, sorry.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:01.100
- No problem.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.050
But one of the things that
actually recently we've realized

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.000
is that another area that's actually quite important

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.950
and interesting for it
is in privacy and security.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.190
We can actually secure training data
for machine learning solutions

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.400
using the very laws of physics with this,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.310
rather than trying to protect them

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.070
using some expensive cryptographic protocol.

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:25.510
- So, I was confessing to you beforehand,
and maybe you're going to help me out,

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.190
because they said I have
a little bit of extra time.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.790
I was in algorithms class.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.580
The last two weeks, you know, when they do like,
"Here's the special topics,"

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.110
they did quantum algorithms,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.930
and I thought- This was 10 years ago.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:40.170
I was like, "These machines don't exist,
so I'm not going to pay attention."

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.690
Can you tell us what makes
quantum computers different

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.930
than traditional computers, Krysta?

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.570
- Yeah, sure.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.620
First, it's similar to
a GPU processor model, right?

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.740
When you think about programming a GPU
or an FPGA or all of these different chips

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.400
that now sit in the cloud,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.980
we think about it
as an accelerator.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.300
So, a quantum chip would be another chip
in this heterogeneous cloud environment,

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.640
and you would program it
from your classical computer,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.880
so it's really an accelerator,

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.910
and it's a coprocessor model.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.940
So, you're still programming
from your classical computer,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.200
and there's still a role
for a classical computer,

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.080
which is important to remember.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.460
And then, the quantum
computer of course

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:22.850
has different instruction sets

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.110
that are required
to run on it.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:29.770
It obviously harnesses the power
of quantum mechanics, which is very different.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.550
And so, we actually have built out
a whole software framework to allow us

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.940
to program a quantum computer

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.900
and then take advantage
of this hybrid model.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.510
We actually look at developing algorithms, and then
we can simulate these algorithms

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.320
actually in our software stack,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.280
but these algorithms-
We like to look at hybrid algorithms

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.400
that take advantage
of the quantum chip

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.100
for the special sub-routine
that needs it,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.580
so a certain computation

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.070
you might ask the quantum
computer to perform.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:03.060
And then, you perform additional pre-, post-processing,
or continued processing in a feedback loop,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.980
if you will,
with the classical computer.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.410
That's kind of how you can think
about programming,

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.730
especially in the early days
of a quantum computer

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.330
how you could program that model.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:16.280
- That's really interesting,
because I feel like we used the GPU

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.260
for that just fairly
recently to off-load

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.240
all of the linear algebra, for example.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.460
For example, we're going to do
some kind of singular value decomposition.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.440
Go to the GPU; it's super fast.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:33.010
But my concern- not my concern, but my question is,
what kind of things will we be off-loading

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.730
to these quantum chips,

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.420
so to speak, that help AI?

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.990
And you mentioned entanglement and other things,
but I'm having a hard time

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.730
understanding what goes there

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.320
and what goes in the other spot.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.230
- Well, that's actually a really good question,
because that's something that we're beginning

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.120
to actually get a better appreciation
for at the moment.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.940
Because right now, we've got a big
laundry list of different techniques,

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.690
and we're trying to figure out the best way

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.670
in order to interface quantum ideas
with the kind of challenges

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.910
that we face in
artificial intelligence.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:04.340
But to give you some example, one of the things
that quantum computers are great at is sampling.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.830
So, if you ever look at
a machine learning problem

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.470
that requires you to sample
from a hard distribution,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.280
we can more effectively
sample from it,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.200
and we can learn properties
like means, variances,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.530
using quadratically fewer samples
from the distribution.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.800
- So, like Gibbs sampling,
that we use for- - A Gibbs sampling is amazing for it.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.980
Other things, other tasks,
like training Boltzmann machines.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:28.230
We've found some amazing
algorithms for doing that.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.640
Other algorithms -- now, they're special case,
and there's a lot of caveats

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.570
that I don't have time
to get into,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.640
but we can provide
exponential speed-ups

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.090
for things like singular value
decomposition in some cases.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:44.170
So, there are actually some major improvements
that we can end up getting for important tasks.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.070
- Let's get down to the nitty-gritty,

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.110
because you've talked a little bit
about some of the things.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:53.410
Are there some machine learning algorithms
that you've been able to already speed up

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.030
using quantum computers?

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.160
I'm trying to figure out how they will
improve machine learning algorithms.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:04.070
And the algorithms that I learned, for example,
were like decision trees, SVMs, neural networks.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.520
How will they help out in that regard, Krysta?

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.490
- Yeah, so building
on what Nathan just said,

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.100
you have things-

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.150
So, we can look at algorithms
like deep learning.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.490
- OK.
- Training Boltzmann machines,

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.140
as Nathan just mentioned Perceptrons

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.760
is another area we've looked at.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.390
You can look at SVMs. You can take many
of your more traditional,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.290
classical machine learning models,

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:30.560
and we can look at achieving
say quadratic or,

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.340
as Nathan said,
in some cases

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.990
exponential speed-ups
on the quantum computer.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.160
So, many of these models
translate over.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.880
But with that, I think a really exciting
and promising direction

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.800
is to say not just,

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:47.360
"Can we take the algorithms we have today

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.050
and run them on our quantum computer?"

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:52.620
But can we actually use something
that's quantum mechanical,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.100
take advantage of quantum-ness,

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.200
to build better models,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.200
to get better accuracy,
better performance?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.260
And what does that mean?

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.970
You know, what does
that quantum-ness mean?

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:07.600
So, in fact, we've been looking at --
in recurrent neural networks, as an example.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.440
In small cases, we've actually simulated
those, brought in quantum-ness to the model --

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.890
so, quantum terms to the model

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.630
- and you can actually see
that the accuracy can increase.

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:25.430
So, this is evidence that really using
a quantum Hamiltonian as model and quantum

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:30.530
terms could actually help
the modeling of data

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.450
over classical methods
we have today.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.510
- So, you mentioned quantum
Hamiltonian and quantum terms,

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.200
and I'm like, those probably mean
something very specific.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.210
Can you help me out, Nathan?

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:41.450
- Yeah, sure, no problem.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.120
For example, let's imagine
what you're looking at is

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.370
you're looking at
a Boltzmann machine, OK?

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.420
A Boltzmann machine, you can imagine,
is just an energy-based system

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.710
that provides an energy penalty

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.570
for different configurations

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:59.220
that you don't want
for the neural network.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.870
If you include quantum terms in this,
what this allows you to do is this allows

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.140
you not actually give energies

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:09.170
directly to an individual configuration

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:12.310
but rather superpositions
of different configurations.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.670
What this allows you to do
is this allows you

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.740
to actually penalize
quantum distributions

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.500
and quantum states
that describe the system,

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.360
and this gives you
some non-local effects

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.690
that are very difficult
for you to be able to emulate

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.090
with a standard
Boltzmann machine.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.420
One of the things that
we've found is by including

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.670
these quantum terms in there,

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.770
we can not only dramatically increase

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.510
the accuracy
for classical tasks,

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.020
but we can actually build

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.450
fundamentally new classes
of neural networks

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.300
that can learn off
of quantum evidence.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.730
- I mean, that's impressive.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:48.540
Because you mentioned our implementation
has the hardware and the software.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.390
What is this software?

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.000
Is this something we've already built?

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.320
Can you talk a little bit to that?

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:58.560
- Yeah, so on the software side,
we're building out the full software stack, right?

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.500
Everything from the high-level
programming language through the compilers,

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.590
the optimizers, the translators,

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.940
everything you need to map
that algorithm down

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.460
to the hardware level

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.930
and then actually
run the machine.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:15.250
So, then you need device drivers,
and you have to control- You know, in the back

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:16.480
we've been showing pictures

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.380
of what this device looks
like on these screens.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:23.320
This is not your
standard-looking device, right?

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:28.450
We have a lot of different boxes in the lab
that need to be controlled to empower this device,

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:30.890
and that also has to be taken

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.780
into account
in the software stack.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:35.220
So, we actually have this
full software stack

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:38.190
that allows you to run
the quantum hardware.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:42.400
And then, in addition, you also want
to be able to test and debug

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.340
before you run something
on expensive quantum hardware, right?

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:48.030
- Sure.
- So, we want to find the errors in the algorithms,

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.600
and we want to simulate these models
as we've been discussing

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.340
for small machine
learning models on small data.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:58.780
We want to simulate that before we run it
to- you know, pass it to real hardware,

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.530
so we can find the bugs,
we can optimize the circuits.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:06.130
There have been cases where we've actually
found huge algorithmic improvements

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:07.820
by running it in advance,

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:09.190
compiling it in advance,

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.620
looking at the circuit,
the gate operations.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.040
You know, classically, we have AND,
OR, NOT, EXOR, things like that.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:19.450
Quantumly, we have another instruction set,
and we actually look at those sequences

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.700
and try to compress it
so we get faster run times.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.280
We can do all of that by using
the software, programming the algorithms,

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.780
running it in the simulator,
looking at it, optimizing it,

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:33.190
and doing a consistent
loop kind of

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:35.150
with the software layer

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:37.290
and get orders
of magnitude improvement,

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:38.870
actually, in run times.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:39.430
- That's impressive.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:43.940
So, effectively, these machines
have their own kinds of instructions.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:49.370
I'm trying to imagine the interface
between classical implementations,

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:51.460
because usually when
I'm doing something in a GPU,

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.670
I load up a bunch of data,
I say, "Here ya go, load this up,"

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:56.310
and then I say, "Do these things."

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.860
Is it- the analogy hold
for quantum computers?

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.040
- Fundamentally, yeah.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.590
Yeah, actually,
the analogy's exactly the same.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:07.990
But beyond that first little level,
that's where everything starts breaking down,

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.930
because in order to really actually
leverage the quantum computer,

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.680
you really have to take
advantage of quantum idiosyncrasies.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:19.540
For example, really, you can actually view
a quantum computer as a giant interference device,

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:22.860
where you get every path
that the data could take

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.970
in the data processing

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:29.790
and then cause these paths
to interfere with each other.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:31.920
And what you want to do, like waves,
is you want to be able

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.670
to actually get the bad outcomes

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:36.250
and the good outcomes
to have the right amplitudes

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.240
so that you can amplify on that.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.220
So, in many ways, it's actually
about wave interference, the programming model.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:48.230
At a micro level, it's very difficult to program,
but when you take a step back and look at it

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.800
through a software
stack perspective,

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.470
it's actually not
that much different.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:57.810
- So, the code I'm looking at, I mean, what is it-
Is it like the COBOL of yesteryear, but just for-

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:59.170
[ Laughter ]
I mean, I'm trying to understand.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:01.280
Because in the end,
we all write code.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.290
- That's right.
- What does it look like

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:06.640
when I'm writing these machine
learning algorithms to sample?

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.510
Because it seems like you're doing-
the way you're describing it

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:11.560
is you're doing a lot of things at once

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.220
and then amplifying the good
and de-amplifying the bad.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.200
How do you do that in code?

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:20.720
- Well, I think part of the beauty of this
is that underneath quantum mechanics,

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:22.240
it's linear algebra.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.670
- OK.
- And ultimately, the state vector is a vector,

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:28.510
and our operations
are actually all matrices.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:30.150
They're unitary matrices.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.530
They have to be reversible.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.360
And so, actually in the code,
what you're really writing-

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.650
And we provide some higher
level of abstraction for this,

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.250
but you're really writing
a sequence of operations,

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:43.130
which are matrices being
applied to a state vector.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:49.590
And so, at some level, it looks like you have
some variable that stores your state vector,

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.440
and then it looks like- You know,

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.320
you just type the name of the operation,

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:57.000
which behind the scenes
has a matrix that represents it.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:02.210
So, you have a qubit type, and you have
a classical integer type,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:04.930
and so you have some different types in your system.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.600
But ultimately, you still...

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:13.490
Our language, for example, is embedded in F#,
so it looks like a standard, functional-style language.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.390
And you take that vector,
which you now write, you know, "ket."

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:20.980
A quantum vector we represent
as a ket vector.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.190
- Cool.
- And that ket,

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:25.180
then you can apply
an operation to it,

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.170
and you just write the sequence
of operations you want to apply.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:31.570
And then, on top of that, if you want to do kind
of these more complex things --

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:35.310
like you want to do something
like the Fourier transform,

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.980
which you get a speed-up
quantum mechanically --

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.230
you can ask the library.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:47.480
We have a library of different arithmetic
and scientific functions and special quantum functions

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.540
that then you can say, you know,

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.840
"I'm just going to type the name
of that library function

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.630
and apply it to my state,
my system."

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.990
And so, it actually
looks much more elegant,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:02.150
if you will, than one might think at the surface.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:03.970
- That's impressive, right?

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.960
And it's fun that you're using F#,
which is a tremendous language,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.280
because I saw something-

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:10.720
Because parallelization, for example,

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.680
was one of the harder things
to do, and I saw F#

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.750
had these sort of
parallel blocks

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:15.950
where things could just happen.

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:18.460
Is there the same kind of blocks
for quantum computing?

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:22.160
You say, "This part is for the quantum
computer, then put the result here"?

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.200
Is that the kind of thing
you're doing, Nathan?

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:27.070
- Well, yeah, actually,
that's entirely what's going on.

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:33.880
Because say you take a look at training
a neural network using a quantum computer.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:38.920
What you would do in that particular case
is you would use the quantum computer in order

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:40.560
to find the expectation values

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.780
that you need for the gradients
of the weights.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.980
And so, once you have that information,
then you pass it back

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:48.370
to the ordinary computer,
aggregate that,

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:49.830
and update your model stored

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.550
in the ordinary
computer for that.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:53.590
So, actually, you can view all of that.

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:57.540
It just is inside a classical loop,
where you're just off-loading this one function,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.740
saying, "Sample from the distribution

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:01.080
and compute the average."

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.380
That part is the only thing

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.140
that the quantum
computer cares about,

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.000
so we can really view this
inside a larger classical framework

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.490
that actually executes
a majority of the algorithm.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:12.200
- See, maybe I've been thinking about this wrong,
because I thought that classical machines

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:14.900
would eventually be replaced
by quantum machines.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:16.240
Is that really the case?

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:20.930
- Yeah, we don't think of it quite
as a replacement but maybe augmentation.

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:27.120
We're going to augment our classical machines
and our cloud ecosystem with a quantum chip,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.360
and it's not the case
that we're going to start

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.460
using a quantum computer to do email

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.760
or use Word or something.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:41.830
We're going to ask the quantum computer
to really do the hard things for classical computers.

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:45.570
- And you're just sort of off-loading
the things that the quantum computers are good at?

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:46.270
- Yeah.
- Exactly.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:46.740
- That's awesome.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.260
So, why start with artificial intelligence?

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.450
Why is that such a good target
for quantum computing?

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:55.450
- Well, there's a couple of reasons
why it's a great target.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:59.370
Actually, I would say that if you take
a look at the grand scheme of things,

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:01.870
the first most natural fit, believe it or not,

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.750
for a quantum computer
is simulating physical systems.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.930
Because in some sense, the quantum computer
is a quantum system in and of itself,

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.860
so you don't have to get it to bend
over backwards to address these things.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:14.740
But the second-tier applications,
the ones that are just slight more

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.020
or less removed
from that,

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:18.670
are actually very naturally
artificial intelligence applications

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:21.290
because of the fact that basically, quantum computers

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:22.940
are extremely good
at two things.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:26.930
They can store very
high-dimensional vectors extremely cheaply.

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:29.790
With a hundred quantum bits,
you have a vector of dimension

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:32.130
2^100 that you're representing
inside the system.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.050
- That's right, because there's
like three different states?

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:35.680
Am I remembering this right for qubits?

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:41.500
- Well, every qubit fundamentally can actually be
in an infinite number of states at the same time,

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.590
but there's basically two
components that you can do.

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:47.110
So, it's a vector in R2 that's unit length.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:48.420
That's one way
you can think about it.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:57.410
The basic idea
behind the advantages are that,

00:16:57.410 --> 00:16:58.800
and also you have the ability

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.780
to actually kind of use interference

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:02.150
in particular ways

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:03.660
in order to sample
from distributions

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:05.570
that would be very,
very difficult to do.

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:08.150
For example, we can sample
from Gibbs distributions

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.030
very quickly using these things.

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:12.520
- This is the second time
you've mentioned interference.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:16.480
Maybe can you help me understand
what's actually going on, Krysta,

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:19.010
with interference?
- Yeah.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:24.280
Maybe let's take a step back and think about maybe like
a tide pool or- - What?

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:25.220
- A pool, right?

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:28.720
You have a small amount of water,
and you put your finger in it,

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.680
and it creates these ripples, waves.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:37.150
And now, if I do that somewhere else
in that pool, these waves collide.

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:42.340
And in fact, when you do this, you'll
note that some disappear, and some actually get bigger.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:45.030
- Right.
- And we actually take advantage

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.450
of this wave amplification

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:49.370
in our quantum
algorithm design.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:53.750
So, the whole idea is to get
the good solutions to amplify

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:56.380
and those waves
to get bigger and bigger.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:58.740
And then the bad solutions, hopefully,

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:03.070
they interfere and actually
disappear or get smaller,

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:06.070
so we would reduce
their amplitudes.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:09.880
And the whole idea with quantum
computing, and something to note around the algorithms,

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.140
is everything's probabilistic, right?

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:17.800
So at the end, we measure the system,
and we get some result with a probability.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:20.780
It's probabilistically given to us.

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.650
So, this idea of amplification.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:26.460
We're amplifying these waves, we get these high waves,
and we'll get one of those waves

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:27.890
back at the end

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:29.980
of the computation
with a probability

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.160
related to its height
or its amplitude.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:37.060
- And that's what allows us
to sort of do all these computations at once

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.130
and have the one just surface
to the top that we want?

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:43.090
- Yeah, that's how we can kind of use it
as a sieve in order to be able to get the samples

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:44.820
that we want from the distribution

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.720
and never see the samples
that aren't informative.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:51.470
- So, you mentioned there was a difference
between our quantum systems and others.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.380
You mentioned topological
quantum computing.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:56.820
What does that mean, and what makes it different?

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.760
- Yeah, so with the type of qubit we're looking at,

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:05.480
the idea is to really start with a much
more solid foundation, if you will.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:10.420
Imagine you're trying to build
a really tall building, and you do this on stilts.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:13.070
Well, you might not get
to a very tall building, right?

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:15.140
Depending on the goodness
of your stilts.

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:20.570
But imagine instead you put
this really good, solid concrete foundation.

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:24.540
Then, you can build to
pretty large scale buildings.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.960
So, I like to think about what we're doing
as building a better foundation.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:32.680
We're trying to build a qubit that's going
to be far more robust, more scalable,

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:34.850
less prone to error.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:39.530
It's going to be much more resistant
to the kind of noise we have in our environment,

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:41.990
and because of that, at its core,

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:44.760
it's just this better foundational qubit

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.620
to build the whole system from.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:51.440
And with this better qubit, our qubit
in particular is very robust to noise.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.730
It's actually encoded in a way
that it's non-locally encoded,

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:58.480
so the information actually
isn't stored all in one place.

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:04.910
It's stored in a distributed
way across several what we call quasiparticles.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:10.040
But by doing this, it's more robust,
and we take advantage of that, then.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:14.460
We can actually get away
with far fewer physical resources

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.740
to build a large quantum computer

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:19.240
than you would for these
other types of qubit systems.

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:23.930
It could be orders
of magnitude fewer resources.

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:28.730
It could be up to 1,000
or 10,000 times fewer physical qubits to run

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:33.470
and build a quantum computer to run some of these
very large-scale algorithms.

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:37.990
- So, you don't need a lot of qubits
to run these types of systems.

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:39.000
Is that right?

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.960
- Well, one of the things,
following up on what Krysta was saying,

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:44.510
that it's important
to make a distinction

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:48.580
between is that when we talk about qubits,
there's two different types.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.800
There's the physical qubits,
that have errors, that have imperfections.

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:53.660
- Sure.
- And then there's the logical qubits,

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:55.200
which are sort of the qubits

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:57.020
that we refine using error correction

00:20:57.020 --> 00:20:58.730
out of those physical qubits.

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:05.270
In terms of logical qubits, with about 100 logical
qubits, we could change the world.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:06.750
No question about it.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:11.370
But in terms of the number of physical qubits,
that depends on what type of hardware you're using.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:15.010
With the stuff that we're proposing doing,
we might be able to do something

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:17.140
around the scale of 1,000 qubits,

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:18.660
as opposed to a few million

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.220
or a few billion with other technologies.

00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:23.550
- That's amazing.
I mean, I remember when, like,

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.730
two qubits were losing state
10 years ago,

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:27.600
so it's pretty cool
how far we advanced.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.970
So, what's next for you
and your work on quantum computing?

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:32.210
What's the future, Krysta?

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:38.310
- Well, so we're really continuing to see
what else can we do with a quantum computer,

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:41.060
so really diving into other areas of AI

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:44.160
and ML, really looking
at quantum heuristics.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:50.380
You know, in the classical world, we use
a lot of heuristics in terms of things we actually run,

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:53.970
say, in Bing or in other applications.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.950
But to run such heuristics,
you have to test them on a real device,

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:00.380
and we're limited
in that size right now.

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:03.460
But we're starting to look
at what can we do with quantum

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.690
heuristics for machine learning,

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:08.310
and we run that
in our simulator in advance.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:13.830
And again, that plays to the role of software
and how important that is for testing in advance.

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.490
So, we're looking at quantum heuristics.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.000
We're obviously continuing
to push on the hardware

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:21.450
and demonstrate a scalable
topological quantum computer.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:25.000
And we're also just looking
for other great applications.

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:28.140
You know, we're always looking
for the next big thing

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:30.620
for a quantum
computer to run on,

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:32.810
so we continue to look
for great ideas there.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:33.820
- Awesome.
What about you, Nathan?

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.690
- Well, one of the things
that I'm most passionate about, Seth, is that...

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:39.030
I gotta echo what Krysta's saying.

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:42.840
We're only at the beginning of actually understanding
what we can do with this technology,

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:44.520
but one of the things
that I really would like

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:46.300
to use quantum artificial intelligence

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:48.350
for is actually to understand

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:50.180
and design quantum computers.

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:53.640
Quantum computers, even with
a small number of qubits, are very,

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.230
very difficult
to actually understand

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.790
at a micro level
what's going on,

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.580
because of this
interference property.

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:03.690
But quantum-enabled artificial intelligences
can tear apart kind of these secrets

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:05.190
held in the quantum correlations

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.220
that we have a hard time
understanding.

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:10.980
And so, this actually opens up
a possibility of building a virtuous cycle.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:16.140
With a small quantum computer, we get the ability
to actually understand quantum systems,

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.900
which helps us in principle

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.140
build and design
more complex ones,

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:22.130
which gives us
better quantum AIs.

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:25.850
And so, this actually could
end up leading to pretty big implications

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:27.570
across not just the software landscape

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.020
but also the hardware landscape.

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:30.830
- We only have about three minutes left.

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:33.930
I wanted to get into-
because I'm super curious about this.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:37.940
What was the first thing you wrote
for a quantum computer,

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:39.710
the first machine
learning thing you wrote?

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:43.950
And then, tell me about how you
actually went and developed this thing,

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:46.570
because you mentioned there's
these simulators that you're using.

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:48.910
You also mentioned that you run it
on the actual hardware,

00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:51.550
and you mentioned that you were sort of
doing these cooperative systems.

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:54.280
What was the first thing you wrote
that you were just like,

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.800
"Holy cow, this is doing the right thing"?

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.930
Krysta?

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:03.170
- To be honest, the first thing
we wrote were existing quantum algorithms

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:06.040
to really test our software
and see how well it worked.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:10.700
And then, we actually used the system
to improve things in quantum chemistry.

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:15.300
So, these algorithms, you want to find a catalyst
and make a more efficient reaction.

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:19.970
We actually were able, through the software
and better mathematical bounds,

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:22.060
to improve that algorithm's run time

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:25.070
by several orders of magnitude.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:27.880
Namely, by name degrees in the polynomial.

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:29.850
- Wow.
- Which is really exciting,

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.880
and the software allowed us to do that,

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:34.190
as well as better
mathematical proofs, of course.

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:38.670
But on the machine learning side,
I think the first thing we looked

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:42.780
at was computing distance
metrics on a quantum computer.

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:47.840
So, you want to do nearest neighbor,
k-means, or something along these lines, right?

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:54.500
We actually started testing
that as our first kind of path

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:56.580
into machine learning
on a quantum computer.

00:24:56.580 --> 00:25:00.000
- That's interesting, because k-means
is very sensitive to initialization.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.420
Were you able to sort of suss out
better initializations with a quantum computer?

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:07.080
What was it about the quantum computer
that helped with k-means?

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:10.400
- Well, the main thing it actually
ended up helping with k-means is the fact

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:13.900
that quantum computers
are incredible sampling devices.

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:17.510
So, what we were able to do
is we were able to not

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:20.970
just estimate what the individual distances

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:22.710
were very effectively using

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:25.920
essentially quantum-enhanced Monte
Carlo techniques.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:28.820
But we were also able to do
a quantum search over

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:30.880
all of the different
cluster centroids in order

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:32.970
to actually find the one

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:34.520
that's closest in some sense

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.980
without querying all of them.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:39.220
We could query actually the square root
of the number that we required,

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:42.170
and that's actually kind of neat.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:46.580
- That had to be fun, because look, like I said,
when I was studying these things, I was like,

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:47.410
"Oh, these things don't exist.

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:51.870
And if they did, holy cow, things are going to change."
It's awesome to see how they are changing.

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:53.510
Thanks so much for spending some time with us.

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:54.420
We are out of time.

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:56.860
- Thank you, Seth.
- As quantum computers become a reality,

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:58.320
it will be fascinating to see

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:01.960
how they propel AI further,
and vice versa.

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:03.470
Thank you both for your time today.

